          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "Your description of about six migraines a month is not an emergency pattern based on the checks I ran. That is not a clearance, though. If your headaches become severe, change in character, or come with new symptoms, get medical advice promptly.\n\nThe general reference on migraine (*Migraine*, General clinical reference) says migraine is episodic in most people. It describes chronic migraine as headache on 15 or more days a month, lasting more than three months. Based on that reference, six a month falls below that threshold. Only a clinician can say what applies to you.\n\nThe national headache guidance (*Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits*) raises one point worth discussing with a clinician. Frequent use of acute headache medicine can itself keep headaches going. It gives limits of fewer than ten treatment days a month for triptans, ergots, opioids and combination painkillers, and fewer than fifteen days a month for simple painkillers. How often you take medicine for attacks is therefore relevant, and a clinician can review it with you.\n\nI can't tell you about doses or which medicines to use. Those decisions belong with a clinician or pharmacist. The other passages I retrieved covered anxiety, thyroid testing and physical activity, and they don't answer your question.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
