          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is that enough to need something preventive?",
          "answer": "I can't say whether you need preventive treatment. That decision depends on factors only a clinician can weigh.\n\nThe national headache guidance (*Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits*) says preventive treatment is offered when attacks are frequent or disabling enough that acute treatment alone is not enough. It gives a rough threshold of four or more migraine days a month with some disability. It also says fewer attacks can qualify if they are severe, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention. Six a month would be above that frequency mark, so whether it matters for you depends on how much your attacks disrupt your life.\n\nThe same guidance says the aim of prevention is fewer and milder attacks, not no attacks. A 50 percent reduction in migraine days is the usual sign that a treatment is working.\n\nI can't advise on specific medicines or doses. A clinician can look at how disabling your attacks are, how well your current approach works, and your other health factors. If you're unsure, that's a good thing to raise at your next appointment.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
